ch equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n=3.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-4}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3)={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k+1}(2i+3)=({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+2(k+1)+3 & ={{field-7}} \\\\  & ={{field-8}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)$ is true for all {{field-9}}."}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving Sums of Series Using Induction" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048631 :topic/math-academy-id 642 :topic/title "Mathematical Induction"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078463 :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078778 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075709}] :question/math-academy-id "q-214167" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $3$ and the second term is $9$.$a_{n} =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, n \\ge 1$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{n} =-5n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077265} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{n} = 5n - 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077266} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{n} = 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077267} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{n} = 5n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077268} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{n} =-5n - 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077269}] :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077264}] :question/math-academy